to see if you go outside  if you do it learns that day's  conditions are good if you don't it  learns that they're bad  over time it builds up a model of your  preferences  we build up mental models like this for  ourselves and others all the time  if you walk with a friend you probably  know what days they'll want to come out  and what days they want to stay home  that's because you have learned a model  of their behavior and even if you've  never seen a day with this exact set of  conditions you can still make a good  guess  machine learning models do the same  thing  the algorithm takes some input like  weather conditions and an answer about  whether it's a good or bad day  then the algorithm builds up a model  from a series of examples  when it's done you can ask it a question  about a totally new set of weather  conditions and it can make a guess about  whether it's a good or bad day now  that's a really basic example to give  you an idea of how these algorithms work  the examples we'll talk about in this  lesson use your personal data as input  instead of weather conditions  they use that data to build up models  that let them guess all kinds of  personal things about you  let's take a look at some of these  in my lab at the university of maryland  we did one of the first research  projects in this space  we wanted to take people's public  facebook information and use it to guess  what their personality traits were  you may have taken a basic personality  test like the myers-briggs test  that's the one that gives you a profile  like intj  these tests measure things like whether  you're an introvert or an extrovert if  you're a procrastinator or a planner and  list maker and if you're open to new  experiences or prefer to do the same  thing to make an algorithm that could  guess these traits from facebook data we  had hundreds of people take a  personality test and then fed a machine  learning algorithm their facebook data  and their scores  the algorithm learned a model of each  personality trait  in the end the algorithm could guess  someone's score on the personality test  exceptionally well  just by examining their facebook data  it was only off by about 10  which is basically how much your score  can change when you take a test  depending on the mood you're in  so even though you don't announce on  facebook that you're an extrovert or a  procrastinator the algorithm can tell  since that first study back in 2012  there's been an explosion of work on  this topic  one of my favorite studies was done at  cambridge university  they wanted to see if they could use  facebook likes to predict a whole host  of personal attributes  so let's talk about the input  on facebook you can like all sorts of  things sports teams movies books bands  restaurants or any page that a person  creates  the things a person likes is a pretty  narrow sliver of all the information  they share on facebook but the  interesting thing about it is that it's  always public  you can't make your likes private so  anyone can get access to them at any  time  if we can use those public likes to  discover private information about  people that's pretty interesting  and that's what these cambridge  researchers did  they used the likes to predict  demographic information like race  religion  gender sexual orientation behavioral  things like if you were a drinker smoker  or drug user they could identify  preferences like if you were liberal or  conservative  they could even tell if your parents  divorced before you were 21.  now you may be thinking that you don't  need any algorithms to connect likes to  some of these attributes  if i like the gop page on facebook  there's a good chance i'm conservative  if i like the jim beam page there's a  good chance i'm a drinker but it turns  out that algorithms don't rely on these  obvious indicators very often  instead they're finding statistical  connections that don't make a lot of  sense to us humans  in the article they wrote about their  work these researchers gave an example  they were able to predict the score  someone would get on an iq test from  their likes  the researchers listed the four likes  that were most indicative of high  intelligence  in no particular order they were science  which maybe makes sense smart people  like science  thunderstorms  the tv show the colbert rapport  and curly fries  now curly fries are delicious but  they're loved by many  how do they show up is one of the  strongest indicators of a high  intelligence test score  the real answer is that we don't know  these algorithms are very opaque  they can give us an accurate answer a  lot of the time but they don't give us  any insight into how it got that answer  part of that is intentional  computer science as a field is only  concerned with whether something can be  computed it's not interested in the  social insights as to why  but i can give you an idea of what's  going on behind the scenes  if you think about it most of what you  like on social media is social  it's stories pages pictures and videos  that appear in front of you because  someone shared it  rarely is it something you go out and  search for yourself  so what we see depends on our friends  sociologists have known for a long time  that we're friends with people who are  similar to ourselves  they call this homophily and it's  basically the birds of a feather flock  together concept  if you're white your friends tend to be  white if you're rich your friends tend  to be rich if you graduated from college  your friends also probably graduated  from college  it's not that all of your friends are  the same as you but rather that your  personal attributes are shared by your  friends more often than they're shared  by the general population  the second thing to know about here is  how things spread on social media  we know from research that viral videos  fads rumors fake news and pages to like  on facebook all spread in online social  networks the same way that diseases  spread in offline social networks we can  take models from the cdc that explain  the spread of the flu  and put them on top of the facebook  network and see exactly how a viral post  will spread  so to put that together imagine we have  someone smart who also happens to like  curly fries she makes a page for them  likes it and then her friends see it  some of them might also like curly fries  and then like the page and it spreads to  their friends now homophily tells us  that since the creator was smart her  friends are probably smart and their  friends are probably also smart  so you get the curly fries page  spreading like a virus through this  smart part of facebook  and in the end when you come along and  like the page the algorithm doesn't know  what curly fries are or iq tests are it  just knows that you did a thing that  other high scoring people did so you  deserve a high score too  essentially the algorithm guesses you  belong to the group of people who liked  this page and their traits are reflected  back onto you  without knowing it the algorithm is  picking up on traces of our social  interactions and using them to learn  more about us  homophily is incredibly powerful when  used by these algorithms and in future  lectures we'll talk about how it can be  used to automatically profile people  even if they have no online presence at  all  and it's not just social media that  allows this to happen  the new york times shared a fascinating  story about these algorithms at work in  the retail space  it starts with a dad in minnesota who  goes into his local target he goes up to  the manager with a target flyer that was  mailed to his daughter  the dad is in a rage you sent this to my  15 year old daughter it's just coupons  for maternity clothes and baby bottles  and diapers she's only a junior in high  school do you want her to get pregnant  and the manager has no idea what he's  talking about because the stores don't  send those flyers out but he promises to  look into it  a couple weeks later the manager calls  the dad back to apologize and the dad  says actually i owe you an apology  because it turns out my 15 year old is  pregnant but target found out before she  told us  how does this happen  well it turns out that target computes  what they call a pregnancy score for all  their female customers and not only can  it tell if you're pregnant it can also  tell your due date  target isn't using social media to do  this  the personal data they have is purchase  histories  if you use a loyalty card or even if you  use credit or debit cards it's pretty  easy for a company to piece that  together into a lifetime history of  everything you've ever bought from them  lots of people shop at target so they  have millions of people's lifetime  purchase histories  target cares about pregnancy because  there are only two main times that  people change their shopping habits when  they move and when they have a baby  if target can find out when women are  pregnant and get them to come into the  store once they can plant the seed that  it might be a good place to shop once  they have their baby  but how do they know someone's pregnant  well it's very easy to tell when someone  had a baby based on their purchase  history the day that first baby is born  they suddenly start buying tons of  things they never bought before diapers  wipes maybe formula  so target can go through their huge set  of data  find all the people who had babies and  look at what they were buying seven or  eight months before that  what purchases tell us a woman is early  in her pregnancy  you might guess a pregnancy test or  vitamins but it turns out it's not any  individual thing  it's combinations of things  one example that was reported is this  if a woman buys an extra bottle of  lotion  a purse that's big enough to double as a  diaper bag but not a proper diaper bag  just a big purse  and brightly colored rugs  so imagine you're in line and the woman  in front of you has cocoa butter a big  handbag and a blue rug and you whisper  to yourself she's totally pregnant  why these things  some make sense but the rug really  doesn't you're not building a nursery  that early in a pregnancy  so why is it predictive  again the artificial intelligence  doesn't tell us why  what the algorithms are doing is finding  statistical connections that subtly  indicate differences between the two  groups  they're not necessarily logical and they  don't apply to everyone  but for the algorithms they can find  many small statistical indicators that  help it guess correctly even if it  doesn't make sense to us humans  it's really important to know that these  are not meaningful correlations in the  data from a human perspective  there are statistical patterns found for  some people in the context of millions  of others that allow a mathematical tool  to separate people into groups  that can be frustrating for people who  want insight because they don't get it  but it also has important implications  for us as consumers  the major thing these examples show us  is that you can't hide from these  algorithms  we may be able to control what we post  on social media or what we buy but we  don't know what our actions say to an  algorithm  you wouldn't know that liking curly  fries would say anything about your  intelligence or that buying things that  aren't about pregnancy specifically  would reveal it  so if there is something you want to  keep private there's no way to know how  to do that when an algorithm is doing  the analysis  also these correlations are not stable  curly fries may no longer say anything  about intelligence  this doesn't matter to the algorithms  they constantly refine and rebuild their  models and don't rely on these  correlations explicitly  for users it means that you can't adjust  your actions based on knowledge about  what was predictive last week because it  may have  changed all of the examples we've  discussed so far are algorithms that  discover things that are true about you  right now  are you an introvert are you pregnant  what's your iq score  but researchers are getting very good at  predicting things that will be true  about you in the future even before you  know it  researchers at cornell weren't looking  at predicting future behavior  instead they wanted to be able to look  at your list of friends and pick out  which person was your spouse or  significant other  to do that they only looked at your list  of friends and which of them knew each  other  they didn't include any personal  information just here are your friends  say we number them one through two  hundred and for each of them which of  your other friends did they know  so maybe friend number one is also  friends with number 27 53 89 and so on  one easy guess about who might be your  significant other is the person who has  the most friends in common with you  basically that friend who's connected to  the greatest number of your other  friends  that's a pretty good guess  but researchers are looking for more  than just good guesses so they came up  with this measure called social  dispersion  at its heart  social dispersion is a natural concept  if you think about your social life  whether it's online or offline you tend  to have pretty distinct social circles  you have friends you grew up with maybe  friends you went to school with  people who do activities with you like  members of your church or sports team  family members  these distinctions are especially clear  for me because i've tended to move every  time i do something different in my life  i grew up in a small town in northern  illinois and i have my high school  friends i come from a big catholic  family and have a whole bunch of cousins  who i'm friends with on facebook  and even though they come from the same  town they aren't friends with my high  school friends because most of them went  to the catholic school  so the only overlap between these groups  are my brother and sister-in-law who  went to high school with me and who  obviously also know the family  then i moved for undergrad and i have  friends there and then i moved again for  graduate school and i have a group of  professional friends  i play ice hockey on a men's team so  there's a group of like 20 guys off in  the corner who are all friends with each  other and who don't know anyone else and  that's fine social dispersion says that  our spouse or significant other is the  person who's connected to the greatest  number of these different social groups  even if they don't have a lot of friends  in common with us  and this also works out for me so i've  only been married a few years my husband  is german and only became a naturalized  citizen a couple years ago  he grew up all over the world not with  me he's nine years older than i am he's  a lawyer he went to different schools  and so we actually have very few friends  in common but he's connected to every  single one of those social groups i  mentioned  he met some family members at a wedding  the high school friend who came into  town for a visit work friends at a  reception and so on  so this is an interesting thing that you  can predict about someone and it  performs remarkably well  researchers found that it's right about  75 of the time which is really  impressive when you're picking from a  list of hundreds of facebook friends  but the people who came up with this  wanted to do better so after they did  that first analysis they thought well  maybe they could collect some additional  data and improve their method  what they found was when they  recollected the data two months later if  they had guessed wrong for you in the  first place you were 50 percent more  likely to have broken up in the  intervening two months than if they had  guessed right  so essentially they were able to build  an algorithm that can tell if your  relationship is going to last while  you're in it and you think everything's  going fine  they didn't mention this in the paper  but they can also tell you who a good  alternative is to your current partner  they could say well it doesn't look like  it's going to work out with bob but  frank over here might be a good choice  other research has specifically set out  to try to predict people's futures  one project followed pregnant women on  twitter and developed a model that could  predict whether they would develop  postpartum depression  on the day a woman gives birth the  algorithm already knows the outcome and  it's right 80 to 85 percent of the time  it does this using data about the style  of people's writings their interactions  and the kind of posts they make in my  lab we were interested in understanding  alcoholism  we looked for people who posted on  twitter that they were attending their  first alcoholics anonymous meeting and  followed their posts after that to see  if 90 days later they were sober or  drinking again  we made sure they explicitly said  it could be a year later they celebrated  one year of sobriety so we knew they  were also sober at 90 days out  it could be that two weeks later they  were complaining about being hungover so  we knew they were drinking  once we had this information we  collected everything they posted up  until the moment they announced they  were going to aa  then we built a model that would analyze  their posts and behavior  at the moment they decided to seek help  our algorithm could predict whether that  help would work and it's right 85  percent of the time  i'm still not sure if this was a good  idea  our algorithm does offer some insight  into how it came up with the answer  it can alert a user that their  difficulties in dealing with stress or  their social circles are indicators that  they may have difficulty in recovery  but it also is deeply personal and could  be used in ways that are unfair  how can you take control over these  algorithms  in most ways you can't  in the u.s you do not own your personal  data and companies do not have to tell  you what they know about you nor how  they're using your data  these algorithms are used behind the  scenes all the time often without our  knowledge sometimes it's for relatively  benign purposes like showing us the most  relevant ad  sometimes it's really helpful like  amazon telling us the items we need to  go with a new appliance we bought  but they were also used in the 2016  presidential election to try to suppress  and manipulate the vote and no one told  us about it until after the fact  i ethically struggle as someone doing  this work  there are great ways this technology  helps people every day but there are  also really terrible uses of it that  invade people's privacy and that impact  their lives  i used to describe my feelings as  similar to how it must have felt working  on the manhattan project building  nuclear technology  you can see the good and tremendously  bad consequences  i used to feel this was a bit hyperbolic  10 years ago but now i feel like the  dystopian path we're heading down shows  that it's a pretty apt metaphor  i don't have all the solutions to the  dilemmas that arise here either but i do  think that understanding the full  picture of how personal data is  collected manipulated and used is a good  first step  and when i think there are important  legal or regulatory solutions i'll point  them out  certainly many things are going to have  to change for the world to remain fair  and just in the face of the power that  comes with big data and ai in future  lectures we'll also talk about steps you  can take to gain more control over your  digital life it'll be scary but it won't  all be scary and my hope is that you  finish this course feeling empowered and  more aware because this really is an  area where knowledge is power  we'll encounter many limits to our  control during this course but the one  limit we can truly combat on 